-Recommended execution order:
+Run on the head node first, then on every worker. The head starts training only when exactly ``NNODES`` live NPU nodes each provide at least ``NPUS_PER_NODE`` resources. The training command runs in the foreground and its exit status is propagated. Worker registration failures and resource waits fail after ``RAY_WAIT_TIMEOUT`` seconds. Worker scripts return after registration; Ray daemons keep running. Keep the worker containers/job allocations alive for the duration of training.
 
-1. First start the script on the **master node** and wait for the Ray Head service to be ready
-2. Then start the script on each **worker node**; worker nodes will automatically register with the master node
-3. After the master node detects that all nodes have joined, it automatically triggers the training task
+Use a fresh dedicated job environment. If a previous instance belonging to this job remains, stop that instance with ``ray stop`` in its container before relaunching. Do not kill all Python processes or delete a shared ``/tmp/ray`` directory. ``ray stop`` is cleanup, not a prerequisite that requires a live cluster.
 
